1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,780
In May 1720,

2
00:00:01,780 --> 00:00:02,920
a French merchant ship

3
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:03,700
approached the coast

4
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:04,560
of Italy.

5
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:05,560
Her captain asked

6
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:05,960
to enter

7
00:00:05,960 --> 00:00:07,320
the port of Livorno.

8
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:09,160
The authorities refused.

9
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:10,200
People had been dying

10
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:12,260
aboard, one after another.

11
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:13,380
Even the ship's surgeon

12
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:14,260
was dead.

13
00:00:14,260 --> 00:00:14,800
The captain

14
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:15,580
had good reason

15
00:00:15,580 --> 00:00:16,620
to fear that something

16
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:17,680
terrible was travelling

17
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:18,460
with him.

18
00:00:18,460 --> 00:00:19,600
He continued west,

19
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:20,880
towards Marseille.

20
00:00:20,880 --> 00:00:21,901
There, he reported

21
00:00:21,901 --> 00:00:22,881
the deaths and handed

22
00:00:22,881 --> 00:00:24,521
health officials his papers.

23
00:00:24,521 --> 00:00:25,881
Yet people and valuable

24
00:00:25,881 --> 00:00:26,841
cargo were allowed

25
00:00:26,841 --> 00:00:28,041
into a quarantine station

26
00:00:28,041 --> 00:00:28,881
on the mainland,

27
00:00:28,881 --> 00:00:30,101
close to the city.

28
00:00:30,101 --> 00:00:31,461
Within months, the captain

29
00:00:31,461 --> 00:00:32,561
would be imprisoned,

30
00:00:32,561 --> 00:00:33,321
the ship would be

31
00:00:33,321 --> 00:00:34,701
burned, and Marseille

32
00:00:34,701 --> 00:00:35,601
would become a city

33
00:00:35,601 --> 00:00:36,561
of the dead.

34
00:00:36,561 --> 00:00:38,041
The most unsettling part

35
00:00:38,041 --> 00:00:38,801
is that the danger

36
00:00:38,801 --> 00:00:39,361
had not been

37
00:00:39,361 --> 00:00:40,861
entirely invisible.

38
00:00:40,861 --> 00:00:42,381
Marseille already had rules

39
00:00:42,381 --> 00:00:43,041
designed to keep

40
00:00:43,041 --> 00:00:44,041
the plague out.

41
00:00:44,041 --> 00:00:45,581
The warnings were there.

42
00:00:45,581 --> 00:00:46,581
What happened next

43
00:00:46,581 --> 00:00:47,141
is the story

44
00:00:47,141 --> 00:00:48,221
of a city that failed

45
00:00:48,221 --> 00:00:48,601
to act

46
00:00:48,601 --> 00:00:49,741
on them in time.

47
00:00:49,741 --> 00:00:50,981
To understand why,

48
00:00:50,981 --> 00:00:51,561
we need to go

49
00:00:51,561 --> 00:00:53,341
back almost a year.

50
00:00:53,341 --> 00:00:54,481
In July 1719,

51
00:00:54,481 --> 00:00:56,761
a three-masted French merchant

52
00:00:56,761 --> 00:00:58,641
ship, the Grand Saint-Antoine,

53
00:00:58,641 --> 00:00:59,341
left Marseille

54
00:00:59,341 --> 00:01:00,941
for the eastern Mediterranean.

55
00:01:00,941 --> 00:01:01,641
Her captain

56
00:01:01,641 --> 00:01:03,521
was Jean-Baptiste Chataud.

57
00:01:03,521 --> 00:01:04,321
Marseille was one

58
00:01:04,321 --> 00:01:05,241
of France's great

59
00:01:05,241 --> 00:01:06,342
trading ports.

60
00:01:06,342 --> 00:01:07,722
Its merchants connected Europe

61
00:01:07,722 --> 00:01:08,322
with the markets

62
00:01:08,322 --> 00:01:09,422
of the Levant.

63
00:01:09,422 --> 00:01:11,042
Ships returned with spices,

64
00:01:11,042 --> 00:01:12,482
cotton, silk, and other

65
00:01:12,482 --> 00:01:13,622
valuable goods.

66
00:01:13,622 --> 00:01:14,602
A successful voyage

67
00:01:14,602 --> 00:01:14,942
could bring

68
00:01:14,942 --> 00:01:16,682
a considerable profit.

69
00:01:16,682 --> 00:01:17,842
Over the following months,

70
00:01:17,842 --> 00:01:18,962
the Grand Saint-Antoine

71
00:01:18,962 --> 00:01:20,062
visited several ports

72
00:01:20,062 --> 00:01:21,642
in the eastern Mediterranean.

73
00:01:21,642 --> 00:01:22,802
Cargo accumulated

74
00:01:22,802 --> 00:01:23,822
in her hold.

75
00:01:23,822 --> 00:01:24,842
Among the most valuable

76
00:01:24,842 --> 00:01:26,562
items were textiles, bales

77
00:01:26,562 --> 00:01:27,502
of cotton and silk

78
00:01:27,502 --> 00:01:29,382
intended for European buyers.

79
00:01:29,382 --> 00:01:30,342
The cargo was worth

80
00:01:30,342 --> 00:01:32,522
roughly 100,000 écus,

81
00:01:32,522 --> 00:01:34,042
an enormous sum.

82
00:01:34,042 --> 00:01:35,022
Some of it was intended

83
00:01:35,022 --> 00:01:36,162
for the Beaucaire fair,

84
00:01:36,162 --> 00:01:37,082
where merchants gathered

85
00:01:37,082 --> 00:01:37,962
in the summer.

86
00:01:37,962 --> 00:01:40,442
Delays threatened serious losses.

87
00:01:40,442 --> 00:01:40,942
One of the men

88
00:01:40,942 --> 00:01:41,602
with an interest

89
00:01:41,602 --> 00:01:42,302
in the cargo

90
00:01:42,302 --> 00:01:43,982
was Jean-Baptiste Estelle,

91
00:01:43,982 --> 00:01:44,922
Marseille's first

92
00:01:44,922 --> 00:01:46,302
échevin, or senior

93
00:01:46,302 --> 00:01:47,342
city official.

94
00:01:47,342 --> 00:01:48,162
The people who stood

95
00:01:48,162 --> 00:01:48,922
to lose money

96
00:01:48,922 --> 00:01:49,643
had connections

97
00:01:49,643 --> 00:01:50,543
to those responsible

98
00:01:50,543 --> 00:01:51,943
for the city's affairs.

99
00:01:51,943 --> 00:01:53,163
That connection would become

100
00:01:53,163 --> 00:01:54,363
part of the controversy

101
00:01:54,363 --> 00:01:56,043
over what happened next.

102
00:01:56,043 --> 00:01:56,543
In the spring

103
00:01:56,543 --> 00:01:58,343
of 1720, the voyage

104
00:01:58,343 --> 00:01:59,723
began to go wrong.

105
00:01:59,723 --> 00:02:00,723
On April 3rd,

106
00:02:00,723 --> 00:02:01,903
a passenger who had boarded

107
00:02:01,903 --> 00:02:03,303
at Tripoli died.

108
00:02:03,303 --> 00:02:04,343
Then, more people

109
00:02:04,343 --> 00:02:05,283
fell ill.

110
00:02:05,283 --> 00:02:05,803
Some died

111
00:02:05,803 --> 00:02:07,323
with frightening speed.

112
00:02:07,323 --> 00:02:08,203
The ship's surgeon

113
00:02:08,203 --> 00:02:09,303
was among the dead.

114
00:02:09,303 --> 00:02:10,143
For the crew,

115
00:02:10,143 --> 00:02:11,863
his death was terrifying.

116
00:02:11,863 --> 00:02:12,243
The man

117
00:02:12,243 --> 00:02:12,943
who should have cared

118
00:02:12,943 --> 00:02:13,663
for the sick

119
00:02:13,663 --> 00:02:14,403
could no longer

120
00:02:14,403 --> 00:02:15,023
help them.

121
00:02:15,023 --> 00:02:15,603
There was still

122
00:02:15,603 --> 00:02:16,363
a long journey

123
00:02:16,363 --> 00:02:17,523
ahead, and the cause

124
00:02:17,523 --> 00:02:18,143
of the deaths

125
00:02:18,143 --> 00:02:18,723
had not been

126
00:02:18,723 --> 00:02:20,363
conclusively established.

127
00:02:20,363 --> 00:02:21,323
According to the later

128
00:02:21,323 --> 00:02:22,263
account of Marseille

129
00:02:22,263 --> 00:02:24,263
physician Jean-Baptiste Bertrand,

130
00:02:24,263 --> 00:02:25,643
Chataud was so alarmed

131
00:02:25,643 --> 00:02:26,843
that he separated himself

132
00:02:26,843 --> 00:02:28,023
from the crew, staying

133
00:02:28,023 --> 00:02:29,103
at the stern and giving

134
00:02:29,103 --> 00:02:30,183
orders from there.

135
00:02:30,183 --> 00:02:31,023
The captain went

136
00:02:31,023 --> 00:02:31,903
to Livorno.

137
00:02:31,903 --> 00:02:32,944
Local officials did

138
00:02:32,944 --> 00:02:33,384
not allow

139
00:02:33,384 --> 00:02:34,484
the ship to dock.

140
00:02:34,484 --> 00:02:35,964
A medical certificate described

141
00:02:35,964 --> 00:02:36,924
the deaths in terms

142
00:02:36,924 --> 00:02:37,824
of a dangerous,

143
00:02:37,824 --> 00:02:39,224
contagious fever.

144
00:02:39,224 --> 00:02:40,544
Whatever its exact wording

145
00:02:40,544 --> 00:02:41,444
meant to the authorities

146
00:02:41,444 --> 00:02:42,204
at the time,

147
00:02:42,204 --> 00:02:42,684
the deaths

148
00:02:42,684 --> 00:02:43,444
were a warning

149
00:02:43,444 --> 00:02:44,984
that could not be ignored.

150
00:02:44,984 --> 00:02:45,984
The ship continued

151
00:02:45,984 --> 00:02:47,224
towards France.

152
00:02:47,224 --> 00:02:48,284
On May 25th,

153
00:02:48,284 --> 00:02:49,804
the Grand Saint-Antoine reached

154
00:02:49,804 --> 00:02:51,464
the waters off Marseille.

155
00:02:51,464 --> 00:02:52,744
18th century Marseille

156
00:02:52,744 --> 00:02:53,764
was not defenceless

157
00:02:53,764 --> 00:02:54,684
against plague.

158
00:02:54,684 --> 00:02:55,104
The city

159
00:02:55,104 --> 00:02:56,104
had spent decades

160
00:02:56,104 --> 00:02:57,704
building a sanitary system.

161
00:02:57,704 --> 00:02:59,204
Arriving captains declared where

162
00:02:59,204 --> 00:03:00,264
they had travelled, what

163
00:03:00,264 --> 00:03:01,404
they carried, and whether

164
00:03:01,404 --> 00:03:01,864
there had been

165
00:03:01,864 --> 00:03:03,004
illness aboard.

166
00:03:03,004 --> 00:03:04,504
Ships from suspect ports,

167
00:03:04,504 --> 00:03:06,024
their passengers and cargo,

168
00:03:06,024 --> 00:03:06,664
could be placed

169
00:03:06,664 --> 00:03:07,804
under quarantine.

170
00:03:07,804 --> 00:03:08,704
For the most dangerous

171
00:03:08,704 --> 00:03:10,144
cases, officials could use

172
00:03:10,144 --> 00:03:11,324
more remote islands,

173
00:03:11,324 --> 00:03:12,524
including Jarre.

174
00:03:12,524 --> 00:03:13,504
Isolation was meant

175
00:03:13,504 --> 00:03:13,844
to keep

176
00:03:13,844 --> 00:03:14,944
people and goods away

177
00:03:14,944 --> 00:03:15,604
from the city

178
00:03:15,604 --> 00:03:16,324
until the danger

179
00:03:16,324 --> 00:03:17,305
had passed.

180
00:03:17,305 --> 00:03:18,325
Chataud had clean

181
00:03:18,325 --> 00:03:19,045
bills of health

182
00:03:19,045 --> 00:03:20,365
from the eastern Mediterranean

183
00:03:20,365 --> 00:03:21,605
ports he had visited.

184
00:03:21,605 --> 00:03:22,505
These were documents

185
00:03:22,505 --> 00:03:23,825
saying that, when issued,

186
00:03:23,825 --> 00:03:24,445
those ports

187
00:03:24,445 --> 00:03:25,325
were not officially

188
00:03:25,325 --> 00:03:26,705
considered infected.

189
00:03:26,705 --> 00:03:27,565
But the deaths during

190
00:03:27,565 --> 00:03:28,505
the voyage raised

191
00:03:28,505 --> 00:03:29,785
a different question.

192
00:03:29,785 --> 00:03:31,285
The Marseille health authorities

193
00:03:31,285 --> 00:03:32,805
had the captain's declaration

194
00:03:32,805 --> 00:03:33,585
and information

195
00:03:33,585 --> 00:03:35,105
about the alarming deaths.

196
00:03:35,105 --> 00:03:36,245
Instead of keeping everything

197
00:03:36,245 --> 00:03:37,125
in the most remote

198
00:03:37,125 --> 00:03:38,985
isolation, the authorities allowed

199
00:03:38,985 --> 00:03:40,745
passengers and valuable cargo

200
00:03:40,745 --> 00:03:41,305
to be taken

201
00:03:41,305 --> 00:03:42,905
to the mainland infirmaries,

202
00:03:42,905 --> 00:03:44,245
known as the Lazaret.

203
00:03:44,245 --> 00:03:45,045
It was a quarantine

204
00:03:45,045 --> 00:03:46,245
facility, but it lay

205
00:03:46,245 --> 00:03:46,865
much closer

206
00:03:46,865 --> 00:03:47,805
to the city.

207
00:03:47,805 --> 00:03:48,905
Historians have connected

208
00:03:48,905 --> 00:03:49,485
this decision

209
00:03:49,485 --> 00:03:50,245
to the commercial

210
00:03:50,245 --> 00:03:52,065
interests surrounding the cargo.

211
00:03:52,065 --> 00:03:53,045
The degree of personal

212
00:03:53,045 --> 00:03:54,745
responsibility of each official

213
00:03:54,745 --> 00:03:55,985
remains debated.

214
00:03:55,985 --> 00:03:56,605
It would be too

215
00:03:56,605 --> 00:03:57,425
simple to pretend

216
00:03:57,425 --> 00:03:58,265
that we know every

217
00:03:58,265 --> 00:03:59,685
conversation, or can name

218
00:03:59,685 --> 00:04:00,686
one person who caused

219
00:04:00,686 --> 00:04:01,786
the catastrophe.

220
00:04:01,786 --> 00:04:03,946
While the city continued its daily life,

221
00:04:03,946 --> 00:04:04,666
people connected

222
00:04:04,666 --> 00:04:05,386
with the ship

223
00:04:05,386 --> 00:04:06,486
kept dying.

224
00:04:06,486 --> 00:04:07,366
A sailor died

225
00:04:07,366 --> 00:04:08,546
after its arrival.

226
00:04:08,546 --> 00:04:09,466
On June 12th,

227
00:04:09,466 --> 00:04:10,206
a guard placed

228
00:04:10,206 --> 00:04:10,946
aboard the ship

229
00:04:10,946 --> 00:04:11,926
died as well.

230
00:04:11,926 --> 00:04:12,846
Porters who handled

231
00:04:12,846 --> 00:04:14,326
the cargo fell ill.

232
00:04:14,326 --> 00:04:14,946
The danger

233
00:04:14,946 --> 00:04:16,326
had not disappeared.

234
00:04:16,326 --> 00:04:17,326
Yet, the response

235
00:04:17,326 --> 00:04:18,466
remained slow.

236
00:04:18,466 --> 00:04:19,946
Passengers were eventually allowed

237
00:04:19,946 --> 00:04:21,166
to leave quarantine.

238
00:04:21,166 --> 00:04:22,226
Goods were still being

239
00:04:22,226 --> 00:04:23,626
handled at the infirmaries.

240
00:04:23,626 --> 00:04:25,146
How exactly the infection

241
00:04:25,146 --> 00:04:26,246
crossed from the Lazaret

242
00:04:26,246 --> 00:04:27,146
into Marseille

243
00:04:27,146 --> 00:04:28,866
is not fully established.

244
00:04:28,866 --> 00:04:30,006
A common explanation

245
00:04:30,006 --> 00:04:31,566
is that contaminated goods,

246
00:04:31,566 --> 00:04:32,826
including textiles,

247
00:04:32,826 --> 00:04:34,466
left the quarantine area.

248
00:04:34,466 --> 00:04:35,686
People and their belongings

249
00:04:35,686 --> 00:04:36,586
may also have helped

250
00:04:36,586 --> 00:04:38,306
carry infection beyond it.

251
00:04:38,306 --> 00:04:39,766
Three centuries later,

252
00:04:39,766 --> 00:04:40,706
no complete chain

253
00:04:40,706 --> 00:04:41,726
of transmission can be

254
00:04:41,726 --> 00:04:43,646
reconstructed with certainty.

255
00:04:43,646 --> 00:04:44,607
By late June,

256
00:04:44,607 --> 00:04:46,087
suspicious deaths were appearing

257
00:04:46,087 --> 00:04:47,507
in the city itself.

258
00:04:47,507 --> 00:04:49,127
A woman on Rue Belle-Table

259
00:04:49,127 --> 00:04:50,867
became ill and died.

260
00:04:50,867 --> 00:04:51,807
Soon afterwards,

261
00:04:51,807 --> 00:04:52,787
a tailor and members

262
00:04:52,787 --> 00:04:54,227
of his family died.

263
00:04:54,227 --> 00:04:55,507
In early July, other

264
00:04:55,507 --> 00:04:57,587
patients developed swollen glands,

265
00:04:57,587 --> 00:04:59,827
buboes associated with plague.

266
00:04:59,827 --> 00:05:00,867
In a crowded trading

267
00:05:00,867 --> 00:05:02,007
city, people died

268
00:05:02,007 --> 00:05:03,487
from many illnesses.

269
00:05:03,487 --> 00:05:04,727
At first, a handful

270
00:05:04,727 --> 00:05:05,667
of deaths in poorer

271
00:05:05,667 --> 00:05:06,827
districts did not force

272
00:05:06,827 --> 00:05:07,827
the authorities to shut

273
00:05:07,827 --> 00:05:08,807
down the port.

274
00:05:08,807 --> 00:05:09,447
But the warning

275
00:05:09,447 --> 00:05:10,927
signs were multiplying.

276
00:05:10,927 --> 00:05:11,987
Deaths at sea,

277
00:05:11,987 --> 00:05:13,307
deaths in quarantine,

278
00:05:13,307 --> 00:05:15,027
sick porters, and now

279
00:05:15,027 --> 00:05:16,547
deaths in the streets.

280
00:05:16,547 --> 00:05:17,587
On July 7th,

281
00:05:17,587 --> 00:05:18,407
the health surgeon

282
00:05:18,407 --> 00:05:19,987
Gairard recognized plague

283
00:05:19,987 --> 00:05:21,267
in sick porters.

284
00:05:21,267 --> 00:05:22,307
On July 10th,

285
00:05:22,307 --> 00:05:23,307
a young physician,

286
00:05:23,307 --> 00:05:25,327
Jean-André Peyssonnel, diagnosed

287
00:05:25,327 --> 00:05:26,167
plague in a patient

288
00:05:26,167 --> 00:05:27,207
in the city and warned

289
00:05:27,207 --> 00:05:28,348
the authorities.

290
00:05:28,348 --> 00:05:28,868
His warning

291
00:05:28,868 --> 00:05:29,648
was not treated

292
00:05:29,648 --> 00:05:30,388
with the urgency

293
00:05:30,388 --> 00:05:31,928
the situation demanded.

294
00:05:31,928 --> 00:05:32,968
Officials may have hoped

295
00:05:32,968 --> 00:05:33,428
the disease

296
00:05:33,428 --> 00:05:34,628
could be contained.

297
00:05:34,628 --> 00:05:35,148
They also

298
00:05:35,148 --> 00:05:35,948
knew that acknowledging

299
00:05:35,948 --> 00:05:36,588
an epidemic

300
00:05:36,588 --> 00:05:38,088
would have consequences.

301
00:05:38,088 --> 00:05:39,008
Closing a port

302
00:05:39,008 --> 00:05:40,528
could devastate trade.

303
00:05:40,528 --> 00:05:41,728
Quarantine could isolate

304
00:05:41,728 --> 00:05:42,588
the city.

305
00:05:42,588 --> 00:05:43,388
Whatever the balance

306
00:05:43,388 --> 00:05:44,788
of motives, the delays

307
00:05:44,788 --> 00:05:46,288
cost precious time.

308
00:05:46,288 --> 00:05:46,708
By the end

309
00:05:46,708 --> 00:05:47,448
of July,

310
00:05:47,448 --> 00:05:49,108
more districts were affected.

311
00:05:49,108 --> 00:05:50,568
In August, the crisis

312
00:05:50,568 --> 00:05:52,008
overwhelmed the city.

313
00:05:52,008 --> 00:05:53,288
Hospitals filled.

314
00:05:53,288 --> 00:05:54,048
There were too few

315
00:05:54,048 --> 00:05:54,728
people to care

316
00:05:54,728 --> 00:05:55,608
for the sick.

317
00:05:55,608 --> 00:05:56,528
Those with the means

318
00:05:56,528 --> 00:05:57,688
tried to leave.

319
00:05:57,688 --> 00:05:59,148
Others stayed behind,

320
00:05:59,148 --> 00:06:00,328
trapped by fear,

321
00:06:00,328 --> 00:06:01,788
illness, or the restrictions

322
00:06:01,788 --> 00:06:03,468
imposed around Marseille.

323
00:06:03,468 --> 00:06:04,308
Escape carried

324
00:06:04,308 --> 00:06:05,428
its own danger.

325
00:06:05,428 --> 00:06:06,168
As people moved

326
00:06:06,168 --> 00:06:07,688
through Provence, the epidemic

327
00:06:07,688 --> 00:06:08,948
could spread with them.

328
00:06:08,948 --> 00:06:10,268
Marseille was increasingly

329
00:06:10,268 --> 00:06:11,149
cut off.

330
00:06:11,149 --> 00:06:12,409
Trade stopped.

331
00:06:12,409 --> 00:06:12,969
The economic

332
00:06:12,969 --> 00:06:13,989
disaster that officials

333
00:06:13,989 --> 00:06:15,489
had feared had arrived,

334
00:06:15,489 --> 00:06:16,549
alongside a far

335
00:06:16,549 --> 00:06:18,229
greater human tragedy.

336
00:06:18,229 --> 00:06:18,709
At the height

337
00:06:18,709 --> 00:06:19,509
of the outbreak,

338
00:06:19,509 --> 00:06:21,469
historical estimates describe hundreds

339
00:06:21,469 --> 00:06:22,909
of deaths each day,

340
00:06:22,909 --> 00:06:24,789
sometimes approaching a thousand.

341
00:06:24,789 --> 00:06:25,669
Bodies could not be

342
00:06:25,669 --> 00:06:27,049
buried quickly enough.

343
00:06:27,049 --> 00:06:28,549
Ordinary services collapsed

344
00:06:28,549 --> 00:06:29,189
under the scale

345
00:06:29,189 --> 00:06:30,389
of the emergency.

346
00:06:30,389 --> 00:06:31,129
One of the worst

347
00:06:31,129 --> 00:06:31,889
scenes was at La

348
00:06:31,889 --> 00:06:33,429
Tourette, where many bodies

349
00:06:33,429 --> 00:06:34,529
lay in the open.

350
00:06:34,529 --> 00:06:35,469
A local official,

351
00:06:35,469 --> 00:06:37,209
the Chevalier Nicolas Rose,

352
00:06:37,209 --> 00:06:39,169
organized their removal and burial.

353
00:06:39,169 --> 00:06:40,209
Workers and prisoners

354
00:06:40,209 --> 00:06:40,649
were brought

355
00:06:40,649 --> 00:06:41,509
in for the dangerous

356
00:06:41,509 --> 00:06:42,729
task, using the old

357
00:06:42,729 --> 00:06:44,009
fortifications as part

358
00:06:44,009 --> 00:06:45,049
of the burial site.

359
00:06:45,049 --> 00:06:46,449
Doctors, clergy,

360
00:06:46,449 --> 00:06:48,209
officials, and ordinary residents

361
00:06:48,209 --> 00:06:49,549
continued to help.

362
00:06:49,549 --> 00:06:50,849
Many exposed themselves

363
00:06:50,849 --> 00:06:52,029
to the disease.

364
00:06:52,029 --> 00:06:53,069
Some died.

365
00:06:53,069 --> 00:06:55,150
In September, Charles Peyssonnel,

366
00:06:55,150 --> 00:06:55,950
father of the young

367
00:06:55,950 --> 00:06:56,910
doctor who had warned

368
00:06:56,910 --> 00:06:58,350
the authorities in July,

369
00:06:58,350 --> 00:07:00,070
died during the epidemic.

370
00:07:00,070 --> 00:07:01,150
The disaster was no

371
00:07:01,150 --> 00:07:02,330
longer confined to one

372
00:07:02,330 --> 00:07:03,090
ship or one

373
00:07:03,090 --> 00:07:04,370
quarantine station.

374
00:07:04,370 --> 00:07:05,090
It had engulfed

375
00:07:05,090 --> 00:07:06,270
a city and spread

376
00:07:06,270 --> 00:07:07,830
into the surrounding region.

377
00:07:07,830 --> 00:07:09,050
The Grand Saint-Antoine

378
00:07:09,050 --> 00:07:09,590
had already

379
00:07:09,590 --> 00:07:10,550
been isolated near

380
00:07:10,550 --> 00:07:11,830
the island of Jars.

381
00:07:11,830 --> 00:07:13,230
On September 26th,

382
00:07:13,230 --> 00:07:13,810
she was moved

383
00:07:13,810 --> 00:07:14,770
into a cove there

384
00:07:14,770 --> 00:07:15,950
to be destroyed.

385
00:07:15,950 --> 00:07:16,770
Acting on orders

386
00:07:16,770 --> 00:07:17,350
from the Regent

387
00:07:17,350 --> 00:07:18,850
of France, the authorities

388
00:07:18,850 --> 00:07:19,930
burned the ship.

389
00:07:19,930 --> 00:07:21,090
The vessel that had returned

390
00:07:21,090 --> 00:07:21,810
with a fortune

391
00:07:21,810 --> 00:07:22,870
in cargo ended

392
00:07:22,870 --> 00:07:23,870
its last voyage

393
00:07:23,870 --> 00:07:24,970
in flames.

394
00:07:24,970 --> 00:07:25,550
But burning

395
00:07:25,550 --> 00:07:26,610
it could not undo

396
00:07:26,610 --> 00:07:27,990
what had already happened.

397
00:07:27,990 --> 00:07:29,690
Passengers had left quarantine,

398
00:07:29,690 --> 00:07:30,510
goods had been brought

399
00:07:30,510 --> 00:07:31,750
ashore, and plague

400
00:07:31,750 --> 00:07:33,470
was spreading through Provence.

401
00:07:33,470 --> 00:07:34,810
The epidemic would eventually

402
00:07:34,810 --> 00:07:36,250
recede, but its toll

403
00:07:36,250 --> 00:07:37,590
remained immense.

404
00:07:37,590 --> 00:07:38,731
Estimates vary.

405
00:07:38,731 --> 00:07:40,071
Tens of thousands died

406
00:07:40,071 --> 00:07:41,451
in Marseille, and the wider

407
00:07:41,451 --> 00:07:42,711
outbreak is often estimated

408
00:07:42,711 --> 00:07:43,651
to have killed around

409
00:07:43,651 --> 00:07:46,191
100,000 people across Provence.

410
00:07:46,191 --> 00:07:47,211
The exact totals

411
00:07:47,211 --> 00:07:48,471
remain uncertain.

412
00:07:48,471 --> 00:07:48,911
What happened

413
00:07:48,911 --> 00:07:50,011
to the captain?

414
00:07:50,011 --> 00:07:51,791
Chataud was arrested and imprisoned

415
00:07:51,791 --> 00:07:52,771
at the Château d'If

416
00:07:52,771 --> 00:07:53,351
on an island

417
00:07:53,351 --> 00:07:54,291
off Marseille.

418
00:07:54,291 --> 00:07:55,411
He spent nearly three

419
00:07:55,411 --> 00:07:56,671
years in custody.

420
00:07:56,671 --> 00:07:58,431
In 1723, a court

421
00:07:58,431 --> 00:07:59,231
dismissed the case

422
00:07:59,231 --> 00:08:00,211
against him and ordered

423
00:08:00,211 --> 00:08:01,151
his release.

424
00:08:01,151 --> 00:08:01,911
The man who brought

425
00:08:01,911 --> 00:08:02,691
the ship home

426
00:08:02,691 --> 00:08:03,751
was released.

427
00:08:03,751 --> 00:08:04,531
The decision did

428
00:08:04,531 --> 00:08:05,071
not erase

429
00:08:05,071 --> 00:08:06,171
the deaths, and it did

430
00:08:06,171 --> 00:08:07,591
not settle every historical

431
00:08:07,591 --> 00:08:08,731
question about the actions

432
00:08:08,731 --> 00:08:10,171
of the city's authorities.

433
00:08:10,171 --> 00:08:11,111
More than two and a half

434
00:08:11,111 --> 00:08:12,191
centuries later,

435
00:08:12,191 --> 00:08:13,431
the wreck was identified

436
00:08:13,431 --> 00:08:15,631
near Jarre in 1978.

437
00:08:15,631 --> 00:08:17,011
Archaeologists investigated

438
00:08:17,011 --> 00:08:18,231
the site, and an anchor

439
00:08:18,231 --> 00:08:19,391
recovered from the seabed

440
00:08:19,391 --> 00:08:20,211
is now displayed

441
00:08:20,211 --> 00:08:20,871
at the Marseille

442
00:08:20,871 --> 00:08:21,971
History Museum.

443
00:08:21,971 --> 00:08:23,132
The physical remains bring

444
00:08:23,132 --> 00:08:24,312
us back to the ship,

445
00:08:24,312 --> 00:08:24,992
but the story

446
00:08:24,992 --> 00:08:25,572
was larger

447
00:08:25,572 --> 00:08:26,712
than one vessel.

448
00:08:26,712 --> 00:08:28,672
Marseille had a quarantine system.

449
00:08:28,672 --> 00:08:30,052
Its officials had information

450
00:08:30,052 --> 00:08:31,532
about suspicious deaths.

451
00:08:31,532 --> 00:08:32,592
The decisions they made

452
00:08:32,592 --> 00:08:33,332
did not prevent

453
00:08:33,332 --> 00:08:33,812
the disease

454
00:08:33,812 --> 00:08:35,032
from reaching the city.

455
00:08:35,032 --> 00:08:36,072
There was no single

456
00:08:36,072 --> 00:08:37,472
moment when the outcome became

457
00:08:37,472 --> 00:08:38,672
certain, and we cannot

458
00:08:38,672 --> 00:08:39,952
know exactly how many

459
00:08:39,952 --> 00:08:41,152
lives different decisions

460
00:08:41,152 --> 00:08:42,132
would have saved.

461
00:08:42,132 --> 00:08:42,972
What we can see

462
00:08:42,972 --> 00:08:43,612
is a series

463
00:08:43,612 --> 00:08:44,472
of warnings,

464
00:08:44,472 --> 00:08:45,412
followed by a series

465
00:08:45,412 --> 00:08:46,292
of choices,

466
00:08:46,292 --> 00:08:47,152
in a city caught

467
00:08:47,152 --> 00:08:48,612
between commerce and the threat

468
00:08:48,612 --> 00:08:49,552
of disease.

469
00:08:49,552 --> 00:08:50,932
The ship was burned.

470
00:08:50,932 --> 00:08:51,352
The case

471
00:08:51,352 --> 00:08:52,252
against its captain

472
00:08:52,252 --> 00:08:53,252
was dismissed.

473
00:08:53,252 --> 00:08:54,092
For Marseille,

474
00:08:54,092 --> 00:08:54,932
the cost of those

475
00:08:54,932 --> 00:08:56,032
months would remain long

476
00:08:56,032 --> 00:08:56,752
after the flames

477
00:08:56,752 --> 00:08:57,500
had gone out.
